  "worked_solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3x(2x+3)}{(x-2)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
